{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1344810-dark-rivers-style-peach","id":1344810,"title":"Dark Rivers Style [PEACH]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-10-30T19:51:14-07:00","id":1344810,"title":"Dark Rivers Style [PEACH]","content":"<br /> Those who swim in the waters of the underworld often find their memories have been swept away by the current. Some take this as freeing, the loss of their old identity leading them to a new life. Some are left empty, wandering across a world that has lost its meaning. A few are stripped of all morals, becoming ravening beasts that destroy all they see.<br /> The Monks of Dark Rivers take this as their founding creed \u2013 No-one knows who they may become, or who they may have been. The monks spend most of their time administering charity to the poor, in hopes that when in some future life the monks will receive the same charity. On festival days the monks hold grand feasts, debauchery clouded from future reproach by drink and herbs, leaving only vague feelings that joy was had. When necessary, the monks fight in battles they judge righteous, always offering mercy before killing lest they one day be killed without similar mercy.<br /> To become a Monk of Dark Rivers is to abandon one\u2019s past life. The monks adamantly refuse to refer to a monk by their past name, and will protect their members to the death from judgment for crimes committed prior to joining. Likewise, they will permit no support given to or from past causes, families and offices. Many members were once criminals and soldiers fought by the monks, offered redemption in forgetfulness. Others were heirs to noble titles, whose rivals could not afford to have them killed, nor permit them to roam free.<br /> <br /> Dark Rivers Style<br /> Weapons: Dark Rivers Style can be practiced unarmed, with a staff, or with a fighting chain.<br /> Armor: Dark Rivers Style can be practiced in light armor.<br /> <br /> <b>Distant Currents Defense</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>1m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 2, Essence 1<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Mastery, Terrestrial, Uniform<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b><br /> The past is a foreign land, connected to the present only by easily-forgotten memories. The martial artist follows this truth selectively, unchanging no matter how much their enemies strive.<br /> Use after successfully parrying an attack. You do not gain onslaught from the attack.<br /> Terrestrial: This may only be used (Essence) times per round.<br /> Mastery: If you already had onslaught, you may remove any onslaught for a 2m surcharge.<br /> <br /> <b>Blocking the Flow </b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>5m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 3, Essence 1<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Uniform, Terrestrial<br /> <b>Duration:</b>One Turn<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Distant Currents Defense<br /> This constitutes a defend other action on a target in close range. If the beneficiary has a wound penalty, add the wound penalty to your Parry for the turn.<br /> Terrestrial: This instead is a simple charm that may be flurried with other actions, the usual defense penalty for flurries.<br /> <br /> <b>Full Life Exhaustion</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>2m, 1wp; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 4, Essence 1<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Mastery, Withering-Only<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b><br /> Make a withering attack with double 8s on the damage roll. You gain no initiative for dealing damage, though you still gain initiative for hitting and initiative break. If this crashes them, they take a -3 penalty to all actions, and a -1 penalty to defenses, until they leave crash.<br /> Mastery: Double 7s instead<br /> <br /> <b>Dark Rivers Form</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>8m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 4, Essence 1<br /> <b>Type:</b>Simple<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Form<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Scene-Long<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Blocking the Flow, Full Life Exhaustion<br /> The martial artist assume a stance of measured apathy \u2013 they know not who they are, so why should their failures weigh upon them?<br /> Gain +1 Parry. Missing with a decisive attack costs 1 less initiative. Reduce all wounds penalties you suffer by 2, minimum 0.<br /> Special Activation Conditions: Dark Rivers Form may be activated reflexively when your wound penalty increases.<br /> <br /> <b>Old Pains Forgotten</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>1wp; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 4, Essence 2<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Mastery<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Dark Rivers Form<br /> Leave crash, resetting to base initiative. Until your next turn, enemies don\u2019t gain initiative break for crashing you. Use this only after a having spent a turn in crash.<br /> Mastery: This refreshes your turn as with Initiative Shift, though it does not allow you to make a join battle roll. This version may only be used once per scene, though the martial artist may continue to use this charm merely to escape crash.<br /> <br /> <b>Empty-Mind Bliss</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>2m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 5, Essence 2<br /> <b>Type:</b>Supplemental<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Decisive-Only<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Dark Rivers Form<br /> This supplements an attack against an enemy with wound penalties. As long as it deals at least 1 level of damage, they suffer a poison with damage (their wound penalty+1)I, B in crash, duration (martial arts+their wound penalty), penalty (-1). For the duration of the poison, they ignore their wound penalties.<br /> <br /> <b>Live for the Moment</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>1lhl, 3m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 5, Essence 3<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Mastery, Uniform<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Old Pains Forgotten<br /> Use after being hit by an attack. Reduce the damage, post soak in the case of a withering attack, by (your wound penalty). If they do not deal any damage, you may make a reflexive withering counterattack with damage equal to (raw damage they dealt)+extra successes.<br /> Mastery: You may pay an extra 5m to replace the counterattack with a decisive counterattack with damage equal to (raw damage they dealt), which does not include your initiative or reset you to base initiative on a hit.<br /> <br /> <b>Treasured Flotsam Strike </b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>3m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 5, Essence 3<br /> <b>Type:</b>Supplemental<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Decisive-Only, Terrestrial<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Empty Mind Bliss<br /> What one man throws away, another may pick up as their heart\u2019s desire. In time, a single person may even play both roles.<br /> Supplements a decisive attack. When you reset to base initiative, reset to (Essence) more.<br /> Terrestrial: This can only be used against enemies with more initiative than you.<br /> <br /> <b>Second Life Mercy</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>8m, 2wp; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 5, Essence 3<br /> <b>Type:</b>Simple<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Decisive-Only, Mastery<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Treasured Flotsam Strike, Live for the Moment<br /> The Monks of the Dark River offer their mercy to everyone they fight, granting those who wish the blessing of a new life. For some recalcitrant sinners, it may be necessary to wash away their past life more directly. <br /> Make a decisive attack. Rather than deal health levels, this attack removes intimacies. The target must choose ties they have, each tie removing 1 level for minor, 2 for major, or 3 for defining, until they have counteracting all the damage. The target of any lost tie is forgotten completely. If they do not have enough ties, they lose willpower equal to the difference, then take anything left as levels of aggravated damage.<br /> Mastery: The martial artist may replace lost memories with new ones. They pick lost ties whose value total to half the damage dealt (i.e., 8 damage would be enough to replace 4 minor intimacies, 2 major intimacies, or 1 minor and 1 defining intimacy). The chosen ties, rather than being removed, have their target replaced with the person of the attacker\u2019s choice. This similarly replaces the old target with the new in all of the target\u2019s memories.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <b>Distant Currents Defense</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>1m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 2, Essence 1<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Mastery, Terrestrial, Uniform<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b><br /> The past is a foreign land, connected to the present only by easily-forgotten memories. The martial artist follows this truth selectively, unchanging no matter how much their enemies strive.<br /> Use after successfully parrying an attack. You do not gain onslaught from the attack.<br /> Terrestrial: This may only be used (Essence) times per round.<br /> Mastery: If you already had onslaught, you may remove any onslaught for a 2m surcharge.<br /> <br /> <b>Lost in Deep Waters</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>3m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 3, Essence 1<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Dual<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b><br /> A river cares not for who swims in it, the sea cares not for what the river brings it. Neither should a person care for minor blows and petty injuries.<br /> Use after being hit by an attack. A withering attack doesn\u2019t add extra successes to damage, and a decisive attack subtracts (essence) dice of damage.<br /> <br /> <b>Treasured Flotsam Strike </b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>5m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 4, Essence 1<br /> <b>Type:</b>Supplemental<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Decisive-Only, Terrestrial<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b><br /> What one man throws away, another may pick up as their heart\u2019s desire. In time, a single person may even play both roles.<br /> Supplements a decisive attack. On a hit, rather than reset to base initiative, lose initiative equal to the damage dealt.<br /> Terrestrial: This can only be used against lower initiative enemies.<br /> <br /> <b>Dark Rivers Form</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>8m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 4, Essence 1<br /> <b>Type:</b>Simple<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Form<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Scene-Long<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Distant Currents Defense, Lost in Deep Waters, Treasured Flotsam Strike<br /> The martial artist assume a stance of measured apathy \u2013 they know not who they are, so why should their failures weigh upon them?<br /> Missing with a withering attack gives 1 initiative. Missing with a decisive attack costs 1 less initiative. Reduce all wounds penalties you suffer by 2.<br /> Special Activation Conditions: Dark Rivers Form may be activated reflexively when your wound penalty increases.<br /> <br /> <b>Old Pains Forgotten</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>1wp; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 4, Essence 2<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Mastery<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Dark Rivers Form<br /> Leave crash, resetting to base initiative. Until your next turn, enemies don\u2019t gain initiative break for crashing you. Use this only after a having spent a turn in crash.<br /> Mastery: This refreshes your turn as with Initiative Shift, though it does not allow you to make a join battle roll. This version may only be used once per scene, though the martial artist may continue to use this charm merely to escape crash.<br /> <br /> <b>Empty-Mind Bliss</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>2m; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 5, Essence 2<br /> <b>Type:</b>Supplemental<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Decisive-Only<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Dark Rivers Form<br /> This supplements an attack against an enemy with wound penalties. As long as it deals at least 1 level of damage, they suffer a poison with damage (their wound penalty+1)I, B in crash, duration (martial arts+their wound penalty), penalty (-1). For the duration of the poison, they ignore their wound penalties.<br /> <br /> <b>Live for the Moment</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>1lhl; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 5, Essence 3<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Mastery, Uniform<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Old Pains Forgotten<br /> Use after being hit by an attack. Reduce the damage, post soak in the case of a withering attack, by (your wound penalty). If they do not deal any damage, you may make a reflexive withering counterattack with damage equal to (raw damage they dealt)+extra successes.<br /> Mastery: You may instead pay 5m to make a decisive attack with damage equal to (raw damage they dealt), which does not include your initiative or reset you to base initiative on a hit.<br /> <br /> <b>Full Life Exhaustion</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>2m, 1wp; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 5, Essence 3<br /> <b>Type:</b>Reflexive<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Mastery, Withering-Only<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Empty-Mind Bliss<br /> Make a withering attack with double 7s on the damage roll. You gain no initiative for dealing damage, though you still gain initiative for hitting and initiative break. If this crashes them, they take a -3 penalty to all actions, and a -1 penalty to defenses, until they leave crash.<br /> <br /> <b>Second Life Mercy</b><br /> <b>Cost:</b>8m, 2wp; <b>Mins:</b>Martial Arts 5, Essence 3<br /> <b>Type:</b>Simple<br /> <b>Keywords:</b>Decisive-Only, Mastery<br /> <b>Duration:</b>Instant<br /> <b>Prerequisites:</b>Full Life Exhaustion, Live for the Moment<br /> The Monks of the Dark River offer their mercy to everyone they fight, granting those who wish the blessing of a new life. For some recalcitrant sinners, it may be necessary to wash away their past life more directly.<br /> Make a decisive attack. Rather than deal health levels, this attack removes intimacies. The target must choose ties they have, each tie removing 1 level for minor, 2 for major, or 3 for defining, until they have counteracting all the damage. The target of any lost tie is forgotten completely. If they do not have enough ties, they lose willpower equal to the difference, then take anything left as levels of aggravated damage.<br /> Mastery: The martial artist may replace lost memories with new ones. They pick lost ties whose value total to half the damage dealt (i.e., 8 damage would be enough to replace 4 minor intimacies, 2 major intimacies, or 1 minor and 1 defining intimacy). The chosen ties, rather than being removed, have their target replaced with the person of the attacker\u2019s choice. This similarly replaces the old target with the new in all of the target\u2019s memories<br /> </div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23597,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23597-autxauty","user":"autXautY","date":". 11-09-2019, 10:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23597-autxauty","name":"autXautY","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-30T20:11:30-07:00","id":1344811,"title":"","content":"Love the flavour, but the power is a bit too strong. A spammable Charm where you only lose only 40% of your initiative on a Decisive is an Essence 3 effect. Negating all threshold successes on a withering is at least E2.<br /> <br /> <i>Edit </i>:<br /> After rereading it's not as overpowered as I thought on first impression, though it's still quite strong. Some thoughts :<br /> <br /> -The main issue is that the martial arts has a lot of universally powerful Charms on the cheap. Martial Arts tend to follow a theme, and usually you won't want an MA if you don't share that theme, but this MA's Charms will supplement any style really well. Basically this means this Martial Arts is always the one you want to take as a secondary if your primary Martial Arts is compatible (and this Martial Arts is compatible with 98% of all Martial Arts since it allows Unarmed).<br /> <br /> -Something this strong might work as a dedicated weapon martial arts, in which case you'd need to remove unarmed compatibility<br /> <br /> -The Charm that lets you keep your initiative when you Decisive needs a willpower cost or a per-Scene limit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25285,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25285-epitome","user":"Epitome","date":". 10-31-2019, 08:10 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25285-epitome","name":"Epitome","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25285_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"754"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-31T05:51:10-07:00","id":1344913,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Epitome</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1344810-dark-rivers-style-peach?p=1344811#post1344811\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Love the flavour, but the power is a bit too strong. A spammable Charm where you only lose only 40% of your initiative on a Decisive is an Essence 3 effect. Negating all threshold successes on a withering is at least E2.<br /> <br /> <i>Edit </i>:<br /> After rereading it's not as overpowered as I thought on first impression, though it's still quite strong. Some thoughts :<br /> <br /> -The main issue is that the martial arts has a lot of universally powerful Charms on the cheap. Martial Arts tend to follow a theme, and usually you won't want an MA if you don't share that theme, but this MA's Charms will supplement any style really well. Basically this means this Martial Arts is always the one you want to take as a secondary if your primary Martial Arts is compatible (and this Martial Arts is compatible with 98% of all Martial Arts since it allows Unarmed).<br /> <br /> -Something this strong might work as a dedicated weapon martial arts, in which case you'd need to remove unarmed compatibility<br /> <br /> -The Charm that lets you keep your initiative when you Decisive needs a willpower cost or a per-Scene limit.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I don't want this to be an armed-only martial art, since it doesn't feel like it fits the flavor to me.<br /> I agree that the early charms are too good as a secondary martial art. <br /> Maybe move Lost in Deep Waters to after the form charm, and either make Treasured Flotsam Strike less spammable, and/or swap it with a later charm. Possibly Full Life Exhaustion, since as a withering charm it's harder to combo (you can, it just costs you artifact stats).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23597-autxauty","name":"autXautY","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-10-31T14:22:55-07:00","id":1345058,"title":"","content":"I like the fluff. It's a very fun organization backed by an interesting set of ideals. <br /> <br /> But I don't see the mechanical theme. Most MAs have a &quot;You use me to do this!&quot; that's obvious to them. Single Point wants you to go first and kill people in one super-decisive each. Water Dragon wants you to wear foes down with a stream of small decisives. Ebon Shadow wants you to jump in and out of hiding. I can't see that here. What are these charms supposed to channel the user into doing? <br /> For example, you've got an Onslaught negator that works when you parry, and charms that soften the blow when you don't parry. Are you supposed to be taking hits or not? <br /> <br /> I also don't get the Mastery effect of the capstone. I understand what it does, but not the narrative way it does it. It feels like Mastery pushes it to an entirely different style, and I don't like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25764-vwllss-trnt-prncss","name":"vwllss trnt prncss","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"244"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-09T07:56:12-08:00","id":1347240,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vwllss trnt prncss</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1344810-dark-rivers-style-peach?p=1345058#post1345058\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like the fluff. It's a very fun organization backed by an interesting set of ideals.<br /> <br /> But I don't see the mechanical theme. Most MAs have a &quot;You use me to do this!&quot; that's obvious to them. Single Point wants you to go first and kill people in one super-decisive each. Water Dragon wants you to wear foes down with a stream of small decisives. Ebon Shadow wants you to jump in and out of hiding. I can't see that here. What are these charms supposed to channel the user into doing?<br /> For example, you've got an Onslaught negator that works when you parry, and charms that soften the blow when you don't parry. Are you supposed to be taking hits or not?<br /> <br /> I also don't get the Mastery effect of the capstone. I understand what it does, but not the narrative way it does it. It feels like Mastery pushes it to an entirely different style, and I don't like that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had intended it to be a mixture of &quot;Make small decisives&quot; and &quot;Survive being attacked by any means&quot; combing to &quot;Wear the enemy down&quot;, but I think I did a poor job of making those feel more omnipresent to the style. I've edited the original post with a revision that I think does a better job of this.<br /> <br /> The mastery effect is intended to make the charm into &quot;Recruit the people you fought by offering [forcing] them to have a different life&quot;, with the non-mastery version be &quot;Open a path to recruiting people by removing them from their life&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23597-autxauty","name":"autXautY","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"286"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}